Title: "Document"
Id: "824dbe21-bbe8-4641-b3d0-b7f225e88539"

Text: "A `.mlg` document is a sequence of top-level groups. Blank lines separate
groups. Lines whose trimmed text begins with `--` are comments. Inside a block,
comments are skipped but a blank line ends the current block.

The structural parser works in two layers:

1. a proto parser reads headings, sections, text literals, nested groups, and formulation blocks
2. the structural parser dispatches each group by its first section label

A heading-only group is not a valid structural group because the group kind comes from the first section label, not from the heading.

Sections have `name:` or `name: inline argument` form. Additional arguments are
indented beneath the section; a `. value` argument is treated two spaces deeper
than its current line. Labels and section order are case-sensitive.

Text arguments are double-quoted, may span source lines, and do not interpret
escape sequences. Single-quoted formulations are unsupported. A multiline
formulation begins only when the entire opening line is `(`, `[`, `{`, or `(.`
and closes with the matching delimiter at the same indent.

A non-text line whose first structural-looking colon follows a section-label
prefix starts a nested group. Formulation operators such as `:=`, `:->`,
`:=>`, and command tails such as `:to{B}` remain formulations.

Top-level items must have an `Id:` section. `mlg check` adds missing IDs before checking."
Id: "6dbf4bc9-68c6-4813-8b64-35a7aac11b86"
